              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for this year, then the Mandarin for date."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 点",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "点 — diǎn — \"o'clock; dot\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "我们明天八点去 wǒmen míngtiān bā diǎn qù — We're going at eight tomorrow."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 点 (diǎn) — a small vertical and bar on top of 口, standing over four dots. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "点 (diǎn) is inherited from Middle Chinese temX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), glossed 'a dot made with the brush'. Its use for the hours is not given there, so how it took that job is not settled."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for times and partings."
